r and scientific  advisors today for a briefing on where  we are to test some of their assumptions  and to think about what might be  necessary there are areas where we think  the government can and should have gone  further already actually which is not  about restrictions statutory sick pay  should be higher because there are lots  of people who are supposed to be  isolating but feel they can't afford to  do so  as a result of the very sensible advice  that chris whittie gave the country  earlier this week lots of people have  voted with their feet have made  different choices about how to spend  their social interactions whether  they're going out socializing the  hospitality industry and others have  taken a hit through no fault of their  own and and support there is needed for  the industry the government could for  example with these covered uh loans that  people have taken out give people a bit  of a repayment break help get their  finances in order so there are things  the government can and should be doing  which they're not so it's not just the  case that we're voting to support  measures in the public interest we are  encouraging the government to go further  to help people are feeling pain in their  pockets yeah but with respect that's  that's all the nice stuff it's always  it's everybody's in favor of giving the  public more money will you be in favor  and will you be voting with the  government if they bring in further  restrictions for example  uh  a further lockdown well i don't think  anyone wants to see a lockdown i hope we  i hope we don't have to go but if the  data tells you that that's what's needed  as scientists are beginning to say this  morning will you be ready to vote for a  further lockdown but as i say trevor i i  don't think that a lockdown would be  necessary if the government takes action  sooner rather than later so we're you  know i'm not trying to be difficult  about this we're going to see the chief  medical officer and the scientific  advice when you say follow the advice  well when you say sooner rather than  later the scientists are saying today  today this morning get on with it  and i i don't quite understand what  you're saying are you what are you  waiting for to say let's get on with it  either you believe the scientist or you  don't well with respect the government  had this advice  on thursday in the in the form of the  sage minutes the government have access  to far more  information options advice than the  opposition do i don't think unreasonably  given that i saw the sage minutes last  night that i want to speak to the chief  medical officer and scientific advisors  so that we can come to an informed view  and i hope that will be today the  question is so we might ask you  something the question is well i think  we might hear something from labor  tomorrow the question is why is it  having received that advice on thursday  the prime minister has said nothing done  nothing well i can tell you why is  because he's hiding from his own back  benches instead of leading and that kind  of weakness in place of leadership  should really concern the public because  i think people out there know  that measures are necessary all they  want to know is what measures when and  how can i plan for them contrast across  that with and what's going on in wales  mark drakeford the first minister set  out a plan that takes wales right into  the new year i dare say people won't  necessarily like  measures who does but at least people  have some certainty they know he's doing  the right thing for the right reasons  and they can see what that path looks  like why don't we have a plan for  england you've told me you're meeting  the  chief medical officer we hope so we do  have a cabinet  so um  let's say 48 hours so we can expect  within 48 hours for labor to take a view  about whether we need further  restrictive measures or not i hope so if  the prime minister isn't prepared to  lead we've shown tha